Reasoning: Rapid leader changes with write timeouts usually mean missed heartbeats from slow disk fsync on the leader, not a network split. Probe raft status first to see term churn and which node keeps losing leadership.
<|call:check_raft_status|> {"cluster": "etcd-prod"}
System Response: all 5 members reachable; current_term=341 leader=etcd-3 healthy=true; no errors reported
Recovery reasoning: A point-in-time check looks healthy, but term 341 with 7 elections in 4 min means it is actively flapping. The instantaneous 'healthy' hides the churn, so I check WAL fsync latency because slow commits starve heartbeats and trigger re-elections.
<|call:query_metrics|> {"query": "etcd_disk_wal_fsync_duration_seconds p99", "window": "5m"}
System Response: etcd-3 p99 wal_fsync=1.8s (other members ~0.012s); the spikes line up exactly with leader_changes timestamps
Recovery reasoning: etcd-3 fsyncs 150x slower so the disk-bound leader cannot heartbeat in time. Do not restart it blindly mid-quorum; move leadership to a healthy node to stop churn immediately, then the bad disk can be drained.
<|call:transfer_leadership|> {"cluster": "etcd-prod", "from": "etcd-3", "to": "etcd-1"}
System Response: leadership moved to etcd-1; leader_changes_seen_total flat for 90s; write p99 back to 22ms
Resolution: Root cause was 1.8s WAL fsync on etcd-3's degraded disk starving heartbeats and forcing re-elections; transferring leadership to a healthy node stopped the flapping while preserving quorum (no member removed).

Reasoning: Two primaries after a network blip is classic split-brain. Patroni's source of truth is the DCS leader lock, so before touching any node I confirm which node legitimately holds the etcd leader key and which one is the impostor.
<|call:query_kv_store|> {"store": "etcd", "key": "/service/pg-orders/leader"}
System Response: key /service/pg-orders/leader = pg-a; ttl=18s; but pg-c still reports role=primary in its local Patroni REST
Recovery reasoning: The DCS says pg-a is the rightful leader while pg-c kept its old primary role after losing DCS access during the blip. I must not just kill pg-c; first find whether pg-c accepted writes pg-a never saw, to avoid silently dropping committed orders.
<|call:compare_replica_state|> {"nodes": ["pg-a", "pg-c"], "metric": "last_committed_lsn"}
System Response: pg-a lsn=0/8C40A218 (matches DCS leader history); pg-c lsn=0/8C402B90 with 47 local commits diverged after the blip
Recovery reasoning: pg-c has 47 divergent commits off the leader timeline; reinitializing it will discard them, so I export those rows first, then rebuild pg-c from the DCS-authoritative leader to restore a single writer.
<|call:repair_replica|> {"target": "pg-c", "action": "export_divergent_then_reinit_from", "source": "pg-a", "diverged_range": "0/8C402B90-0/8C40A218"}
System Response: 47 diverged rows exported to recovery_pg_c.sql; pg-c reinitialized as replica of pg-a; cluster now single-primary
Resolution: Root cause was pg-c retaining a stale primary role after losing DCS quorum during the blip; rebuilding it from the DCS-authoritative leader restored the single-writer invariant while the 47 divergent orders were preserved for manual reconciliation instead of being dropped.

Reasoning: Cassandra resolves conflicts by cell timestamp (last-write-wins). A node with a skewed clock stamps writes in the past (they lose) or the future (they shadow real updates). cass-07 just rebooted, so check its NTP offset first.
<|call:check_ntp_offset|> {"host": "cass-07"}
System Response: ntpd active; stratum=3; offset reported = 0.4ms, looks in sync
Recovery reasoning: ntpd claims sync, but it only measures against its configured peer which may itself be wrong, and a step could already have happened at boot. The symptom is timestamp-shaped, so I compare actual wall clocks across coordinators rather than trusting ntpd's self-report.
<|call:compare_replica_state|> {"nodes": ["cass-05", "cass-06", "cass-07"], "metric": "wall_clock_now"}
System Response: cass-05=14:22:031 cass-06=14:22:033 cass-07=14:25:902 -- cass-07 is about 3m9s AHEAD
Recovery reasoning: cass-07 is 189s in the future despite ntpd reporting sync; it picked up a bad upstream after reboot, so its future-timestamped writes shadow correct values. Slew it back (not a backward step, which would break timestamp monotonicity) and point it at a known-good peer.
<|call:sync_clock|> {"host": "cass-07", "mode": "slew", "peer": "ntp-internal-1"}
System Response: offset converging; after 4m cass-07 within 5ms of peers; new reads return current rows; future-dated cells will lapse by TTL
Resolution: Root cause was cass-07 syncing to a bad NTP source after reboot, leaving it 189s ahead so its future-timestamped writes shadowed real data; slewing it back into agreement restored read correctness without stepping the clock backward (preserving timestamp monotonicity).

Reasoning: Two workers running the same job means the lock failed to provide mutual exclusion. The 90s job outlives the 30s TTL, so the lease likely expired mid-run and a second worker grabbed it. Inspect the lease lifecycle for last night's run.
<|call:describe_lease|> {"store": "redis", "key": "lock:nightly-rollup", "window": "last_run"}
System Response: lock acquired 02:00:00 by worker-A token=7f3a; released 02:01:31 by worker-A token=7f3a -- a single clean acquire/release, looks correct
Recovery reasoning: The release log looks clean, but with a 30s TTL under a 90s job the key must have expired around 02:00:30, so worker-A's final 'release' actually deleted whatever held the lock then. I audit every acquire in that window to see if another worker held it in between.
<|call:get_idempotency_record|> {"key": "lock:nightly-rollup", "audit": "all_acquires", "window": "02:00:00-02:01:31"}
System Response: 02:00:00 worker-A acquired; 02:00:31 KEY EXPIRED (no extend); 02:00:31 worker-B acquired token=b912; 02:01:31 worker-A DEL (deleted worker-B's lock, no token check)
Recovery reasoning: Confirmed: the lease expired at 30s, worker-B acquired, and worker-A's release blindly deleted without checking its token, so both ran. The safe fix is a renewal watchdog to hold the lock past job duration plus a token-checked release so a stale holder cannot delete a live lock.
<|call:update_config|> {"target": "nightly-rollup.lock", "patch": {"ttl_seconds": 30, "auto_renew": true, "renew_interval_s": 10, "release_requires_token_match": true}}
System Response: config applied; next run held the lock 91s via 9 renewals under one token; no second acquire; release verified token match
Resolution: Root cause was the 30s lease expiring under a 90s job combined with a non-token-checked release, letting worker-B acquire and worker-A delete it; a renewal watchdog plus token-checked release restored mutual exclusion (fencing the unlock so only the true owner can release).

Reasoning: 5 servers minus 2 should still hold quorum (3 of 5), yet there is no leader, so either a third server is unhealthy or the dead peers are still in the Raft config blocking elections. List the live Raft peer set first.
<|call:list_cluster_members|> {"cluster": "consul-dc1", "type": "raft_peers"}
System Response: 5 peers listed, all voter; consul members shows 3 alive (consul-1,2,4) and 2 failed (consul-3,5) -- looks like a normal 3/5 majority
Recovery reasoning: Serf membership shows a 3-of-5 majority and looks fine, but Raft still counts all 5 as voters, so an election needs 3 reachable voters that will actually grant a vote. I check raft status to see whether the live three are progressing.
<|call:check_raft_status|> {"cluster": "consul-dc1"}
System Response: no leader; consul-1 candidate term=88 votes=2/5 (self + consul-2); consul-4 logs 'rejecting vote: log behind' so it cannot grant
Recovery reasoning: consul-4's log is behind so it will not vote, leaving 2/5 (one short) because the two dead voters are still counted. Autopilot never reaped them. The safe fix is to remove the dead peers so quorum drops to 2 of 3, letting the live majority elect a leader, rather than force-bootstrapping a new cluster.
<|call:remove_member|> {"cluster": "consul-dc1", "members": ["consul-3", "consul-5"], "method": "raft_remove_peer"}
System Response: peers removed; voter set now {consul-1,2,4}; consul-1 elected leader term=89; KV writes succeeding
Resolution: Root cause was the terminated servers remaining as Raft voters so quorum still required 3 votes while a lagging replica would not grant one; removing the dead peers lowered quorum to a reachable majority and restored a leader without bootstrapping (preserving the committed log).

Reasoning: An asymmetric view where one node sees a healthy peer as DOWN is a gossip partition or a stuck generation. Start by querying the gossip state cass-eu-2 holds about cass-eu-9.
<|call:check_gossip_state|> {"node": "cass-eu-2", "about": "cass-eu-9"}
System Response: ERROR: gossip endpoint cass-eu-9 status=DOWN heartbeat_generation=1699 last_seen=612s ago; cannot fetch live state
Recovery reasoning: cass-eu-2 has not seen a gossip heartbeat from cass-eu-9 in 612s though the node is up, so heartbeats from 9 are not reaching 2. I probe the actual gossip port path between them rather than assuming the node is dead.
<|call:check_network_connectivity|> {"from": "cass-eu-9", "to": "cass-eu-2", "port": 7000}
System Response: TCP connect handshakes, but cass-eu-9 to cass-eu-2:7000 shows 100% RST after SYN-ACK -- a stale conntrack entry resetting the established gossip stream
Recovery reasoning: The handshake completes but the established gossip stream is reset by a stale conntrack entry, so 9's heartbeats die in flight and 2 marks it down. Flushing the bad flow and nudging a resync restores gossip without restarting either node, which avoids a ring rebuild.
<|call:check_gossip_state|> {"node": "cass-eu-2", "action": "reset_endpoint_and_resync", "endpoint": "cass-eu-9"}
System Response: conntrack flushed for the 7000 flow; cass-eu-2 received gen=1699 heartbeat; cass-eu-9 now UN on all nodes; hint backlog draining
Resolution: Root cause was a stale conntrack entry resetting the established gossip TCP stream from cass-eu-9 to cass-eu-2 so heartbeats were lost and the node marked DOWN; flushing the flow and resyncing gossip reconciled the ring without a restart (preserving token ownership).

Reasoning: A double charge under one idempotency key means the dedupe check did not see the first request when the retry arrived. Either the key was not persisted before the downstream charge, or the requests hit different stores. Pull the record history for this key.
<|call:get_idempotency_record|> {"key": "ord-55120-charge", "history": true}
System Response: ERROR: 2 records found for key ord-55120-charge (expected unique) -- record A on shard pay-db-3 created 12:00:01, record B on shard pay-db-7 created 12:00:10
Recovery reasoning: Two records for one key on two shards means the uniqueness constraint is per-shard and the requests routed apart, so neither saw the other. I trace the request to learn what routing key split them.
<|call:trace_request|> {"idempotency_key": "ord-55120-charge", "fields": "shard_route_key"}
System Response: req-1 shard_route_key=ord-55120-charge:sess-aa; req-2 shard_route_key=ord-55120-charge:sess-bb -- the session id was appended to the shard key
Recovery reasoning: The shard route key appends the session id, so a retry on a new session lands on a different shard and bypasses dedupe entirely. Fix: route purely by the idempotency key so all replays converge on one shard, with a global unique index as a backstop.
<|call:update_config|> {"target": "payments.idempotency", "patch": {"shard_route_key": "idempotency_key_only", "global_unique_index": "idempotency_key"}}
System Response: config applied; a replay on a fresh session converged to pay-db-3 and returned the cached 200 with no new charge; duplicate window closed
Resolution: Root cause was the shard route key including the session id so a retried request on a new session hit a different shard and escaped the per-shard idempotency check; routing solely by the idempotency key plus a global unique index made replays converge and preserved exactly-once charge semantics.

Reasoning: A worker that lost its lock still wrote successfully is the textbook fencing failure where storage does not reject stale writers. A lease alone is not enough; the write path must enforce a monotonic fencing token. Check what token storage recorded for segment-0007.
<|call:check_fencing_token|> {"resource": "segment-0007"}
System Response: segment-0007 last_write accepted; writer=worker-A token=33; status=OK, write durable
Recovery reasoning: Storage reports a clean durable write with token 33, which looks fine, but the new holder should carry a higher token. If 33 overwrote a higher token, storage is not comparing tokens at all. Compare against the lock service's current token.
<|call:check_fencing_token|> {"resource": "lock:segment-0007", "source": "lock_service"}
System Response: lock_service current_token=34 held by worker-B; previous_token=33 (worker-A) expired at 12:30:06; worker-A wrote at 12:30:12 with token=33
Recovery reasoning: Confirmed: worker-A wrote token 33 six seconds after its lease expired while worker-B already held token 34, and storage accepted 33 over 34 because it never compares tokens. The fix is to make storage reject any write whose token is at or below the highest already accepted, then restore worker-B's data.
<|call:fence_writer|> {"resource": "segment-0007", "enforce": "reject_token_lte_max_seen", "restore_from_token": 34}
System Response: storage now rejects token<=max_seen(34); worker-A replay with token 33 returns 409 FENCED; segment-0007 restored to worker-B token-34 content
Resolution: Root cause was object storage accepting writes without comparing fencing tokens, so a GC-paused worker wrote with stale token 33 after its lease expired; enforcing monotonic token rejection at the storage layer and restoring the token-34 write preserved the linearizable single-writer invariant.

Reasoning: An ephemeral znode vanishing every ~40s with no GC suggests either ZK session timeouts from network blips or two processes claiming the same ephemeral path and evicting each other. Inspect the session bound to broker-4's znode.
<|call:describe_zk_session|> {"znode": "/brokers/ids/4"}
System Response: /brokers/ids/4 exists; ephemeralOwner=0x18ec session valid; negotiated_timeout=6000ms; status=OK
Recovery reasoning: Right now the znode is healthy under one session, but it churns every 40s so a point-in-time check cannot catch it. Two brokers sharing an id would each recreate the path under their own session, flipping the owner. Check the ownership-flip history.
<|call:describe_zk_session|> {"znode": "/brokers/ids/4", "history": "ownership_flips", "window": "5m"}
System Response: ephemeralOwner alternates between 0x18ec (host kafka-4a, 10.0.4.11) and 0x22af (host kafka-4b, 10.0.4.27) every ~40s -- TWO hosts registering broker.id=4
Recovery reasoning: A duplicate broker.id=4 lives on two hosts; each znode creation evicts the other's ephemeral node, dropping broker-4 from the ISR. kafka-4b was likely mis-cloned. Give the impostor a unique id so it stops fighting for /brokers/ids/4.
<|call:update_config|> {"target": "kafka-4b", "patch": {"broker.id": 7}, "restart": true}
System Response: kafka-4b restarted as broker.id=7; /brokers/ids/4 owner stable at 0x18ec for 5m; broker-4 stays in ISR; no further session churn
Resolution: Root cause was two hosts both configured broker.id=4, each recreating the same ZooKeeper ephemeral znode and evicting the other's session; reassigning the mis-cloned host a unique broker id ended the ownership fight and kept broker-4 stable in the ISR (no metadata loss).

Reasoning: 'required revision has been compacted' means a watcher tried to resume from a revision older than etcd's compaction floor -- the watch fell behind and its start revision was compacted away. Check etcd's current revision versus the compaction floor.
<|call:get_mvcc_revision|> {"cluster": "k8s-prod"}
System Response: ERROR fetching watcher state: current_revision=48211904 compact_revision=48205000; gap=6904 -- but the apiserver watch cache reports 'healthy'
Recovery reasoning: The apiserver watch cache says healthy while etcd shows watchers needing revisions below the compact floor, so the cache is masking stale downstream resumes. The real question is why compaction outran the watchers, so I check the compaction interval against the write rate.
<|call:query_metrics|> {"query": "etcd_compaction_interval and etcd_mvcc_put_total rate", "window": "15m"}
System Response: auto-compaction-retention=5m but a CRD reconcile loop did 6900 puts in 4m (hot-loop); compaction ran and discarded revisions the lagging controllers still referenced
Recovery reasoning: A CRD hot-loop spiked writes so the 5m compaction window discarded revisions before slow watchers caught up; restarting controllers would just re-trigger the loop. The safe fix is to force the watch caches to re-list from the current revision and widen retention so transient lag cannot cross the floor.
<|call:update_config|> {"target": "etcd.compaction", "patch": {"auto_compaction_retention": "30m"}, "also": {"apiserver": "force_rewatch_from_current"}}
System Response: watch caches re-listed at rev 48211904; 'required revision compacted' errors stopped; controllers reconciling; 30m retention absorbs the hot-loop lag
Resolution: Root cause was a CRD hot-loop inflating etcd revisions so the 5m auto-compaction discarded revisions lagging watchers still needed; forcing a rewatch from the current revision and widening compaction retention restored reconciliation without mass restarts (no committed state lost, the compaction floor was just relaxed).

Reasoning: A prepared-but-uncommitted transaction holding locks after the coordinator died is a 2PC coordinator failure. I must not blindly commit or roll back, which could break atomicity; first query the participant's exact state.
<|call:query_transaction_state|> {"xid": "saga-ord-77310", "participant": "inventory-db"}
System Response: inventory-db: xid saga-ord-77310 state=PREPARED, holding locks on SKU-9981; coordinator decision=UNKNOWN (coordinator pod OOMKilled, no response)
Recovery reasoning: The participant is PREPARED but the coordinator's decision is unknown, so resolving unilaterally risks desyncing the two branches. I must recover the coordinator's durable decision before acting, so I read its persisted transaction log.
<|call:query_transaction_state|> {"xid": "saga-ord-77310", "participant": "coordinator_log", "source": "persistent_volume"}
System Response: coordinator WAL: xid saga-ord-77310 -> GLOBAL COMMIT record fsync'd at 11:48:02 BEFORE the OOM; order-db already committed its branch
Recovery reasoning: The coordinator durably logged GLOBAL COMMIT and order-db already committed, so atomicity requires inventory-db to commit too (rolling back would desync order vs inventory). Drive the in-doubt branch to commit per the recovered decision.
<|call:commit_transaction|> {"xid": "saga-ord-77310", "participant": "inventory-db", "decision": "commit", "reason": "recovered_global_commit_from_coordinator_wal"}
System Response: inventory-db committed the branch; locks on SKU-9981 released; checkouts flowing; order-db and inventory-db now consistent
Resolution: Root cause was the coordinator being OOM-killed after durably logging GLOBAL COMMIT but before notifying inventory-db, leaving an in-doubt prepared transaction; replaying the recovered commit decision released the locks while preserving 2PC atomicity (both participants committed, never split).

Reasoning: Edits vanishing then reappearing right after a read-replica rollout is a read-your-writes violation: the read after a write lands on a replica that has not applied it yet. Quantify replica lag first to confirm and find the worst offender.
<|call:query_metrics|> {"query": "pg_replication_lag_seconds cluster=profiles-db", "window": "10m"}
System Response: replica-1 lag=0.02s, replica-2 lag=0.05s, replica-3 lag=0.04s -- all replicas under 60ms, well within SLO
Recovery reasoning: Lag is tiny and within SLO, so it is not gross replication delay, yet users see 3s gaps. The window between commit on primary and visibility on a replica can still bite a read issued milliseconds after a write. I trace one affected request to see which node and LSN served it.
<|call:trace_request|> {"user": "u-4471", "ops": ["write_profile", "read_profile"], "fields": "served_by,lsn"}
System Response: write committed on primary at lsn 0/5A12; read 40ms later served by replica-2 at applied_lsn 0/59F0 (behind the write lsn) -> returned a stale row
Recovery reasoning: Even with 50ms lag, a read 40ms after the write reached a replica still behind the write's LSN. The fix is causal reads: require the replica's applied_lsn to be at least the client's last write LSN, falling back to the primary briefly. Enable LSN-aware routing.
<|call:update_config|> {"target": "profiles-db.read_routing", "patch": {"mode": "read_your_writes", "method": "min_applied_lsn", "fallback": "primary_for_ms:500"}}
System Response: router now requires replica applied_lsn>=client last-write lsn else routes to primary; replayed u-4471 flow shows the edit immediately visible; no stale reads in a 10m sample
Resolution: Root cause was round-robin replica reads ignoring replication LSN so a read right after a write hit a not-yet-caught-up replica; LSN-aware read-your-writes routing with a brief primary fallback restored monotonic read consistency without removing replicas (load still spreads once caught up).

Reasoning: 'tocommit out of range [lastIndex]' after power loss means etcd-b2's WAL was truncated mid-fsync: its claimed commit index is ahead of the log it actually has. The 4 healthy members hold quorum, so I inspect b2's log/snapshot consistency before any destructive action.
<|call:inspect_consensus_log|> {"node": "etcd-b2"}
System Response: ERROR: WAL tail CRC mismatch at index 1820291; snapshot says commit=1820345 but last valid WAL entry=1820290 -- log torn by the unclean shutdown
Recovery reasoning: b2's WAL is torn: its snapshot promises a commit index 55 entries past its last valid log entry, so it can never replay cleanly and must not be coerced to start. Since the other four hold quorum, the safe path is to evict b2 from membership first.
<|call:remove_member|> {"cluster": "etcd-meta", "member": "etcd-b2", "method": "member_remove"}
System Response: etcd-b2 removed from cluster; membership now 4 voters, quorum intact; b2 no longer a peer
Recovery reasoning: With b2 out, the cluster is safe on 4 voters. Re-add it as a learner with a clean data dir so it receives a fresh leader snapshot and catches up before promotion, avoiding the torn WAL entirely and keeping it out of quorum math until synced.
<|call:repair_replica|> {"cluster": "etcd-meta", "node": "etcd-b2", "action": "wipe_and_readd_as_learner", "source": "leader_snapshot"}
System Response: b2 data dir wiped; re-added as learner; received snapshot at index 1820360; caught up and promoted to voter; cluster 5/5 healthy
Resolution: Root cause was a torn WAL on etcd-b2 from the rack-B power loss leaving its commit index ahead of its last valid log entry; evicting and re-adding it as a learner with a fresh leader snapshot restored full membership without ever forcing the corrupt log into the quorum (raft safety preserved).

Reasoning: Flapping results at QUORUM mean replicas diverged and the coordinator returns whichever two it reaches first. A replica that missed writes during maintenance with expired hints would be stale. Compare per-replica versions for this cart first.
<|call:compare_replica_state|> {"key": "cart:u-9920", "replicas": "all"}
System Response: replica-A v=17, replica-B v=17, replica-C v=11; QUORUM(R=2) often reads A+B = consistent v17, looks healthy on most reads
Recovery reasoning: Most quorum reads hit A+B and look consistent at v17, masking that replica-C is stuck at v11; when the coordinator happens to read A+C or B+C, read-repair fires mid-flight and the client sees the value flip. Confirm C is stale and why it never caught up.
<|call:get_vector_clock|> {"key": "cart:u-9920", "replica": "replica-C"}
System Response: replica-C vector_clock {A:17,B:17,C:11} trails by 6 writes; hinted_handoff for C: 6 hints EXPIRED (hint_window=3h but C stayed down 4h) before delivery
Recovery reasoning: replica-C missed 6 writes and the hints expired before it returned (down longer than the 3h hint window), so normal handoff cannot heal it. The correct fix is a full anti-entropy Merkle repair to reconcile C from the authoritative A/B copies.
<|call:repair_replica|> {"key_range": "cart:u-9920", "method": "merkle_anti_entropy", "authority": ["replica-A", "replica-B"]}
System Response: merkle diff found 6 missing cells on replica-C; streamed from A/B; replica-C now v17 {A:17,B:17,C:17}; consecutive QUORUM reads stable at v17
Resolution: Root cause was replica-C missing 6 writes during maintenance while its hints expired before recovery, so quorum reads intermittently surfaced the stale copy; a Merkle anti-entropy repair reconciled C to the latest version and restored read convergence (eventual consistency healed with no data loss since A/B held the authoritative writes).
